{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1792923543,"nonce":"xke+EpZfPfunxQMhuOSTAJPdAAk=","target":"1626b90d930d209e848220743e00494408af449d2cdabdddea70f6c0a5337545","message":"A Caribou project mixes Haxe, Wren, Lua, Python and other Zyntax frontends,\nand plugins. Each language imports the others' modules the way it imports\nits own: `import game.hud.Hud` in Haxe for a Wren class, `from\nchimpmonk.runtime.WorldTime import WorldTime` in Python for a Haxe class,\n`import \"game:tally\" for score` in Wren for a Python function. Each\nlanguage's own server sees only that language's files, so it reports every\ncross-language import as a missing module and every type from another\nlanguage as unknown.\n\n`caribou lsp` is one server for the project. It knows every module of every\nlanguage and every plugin's API, as the runtime does. It answers each\nlanguage's editor requests with that knowledge: diagnostics, hover, go to\ndefinition and completion across languages, with no false missing-module\nreports.\n\n\nExtend rayzor-lsp. rayzor-lsp is a Haxe server built on rayzor's front end.\nIt already gives diagnostics as you type, hover, definition, references,\nhighlights, completion, semantic tokens, signature help and inlay hints, and\nreads class paths from hxml, rayzor.toml and haxelib.json.\n\n`caribou-lsp` is a crate in this workspace. It depends on rayzor-lsp by git\nrevision. It supplies the modules that Haxe cannot find on its class paths\nfrom Caribou's registry, and routes the other languages' documents to their\nown analyses. `caribou lsp` starts it on stdio.\n\n\n- **Imports resolve only to files in rayzor-lsp.**\n  - The namespace resolver needs the module's file on disk\n    (compiler/src/tast/namespace.rs resolve_qualified_path_impl).\n  - `source_overrides` apply only to a path that was already found.\n  - `CompilerPlugin` works at the MIR level.\n  - `lsp/src/lib.rs` exports only `run_lsp`, `serve` and `spawn_session`.\n  - So no seam can supply a module today.\n- **Caribou's build macro would not run in the editor.** rayzor runs\n  initialization macros from hxml and extraParams.hxml, but\n  `caribou.Bridge.use()` fails there:\n  - `Compiler.getOutput` is unsupported;\n  - types that an initialization macro defines are dropped;\n  - class paths come back absolute, and Bridge skips those.\n\n  The macro would also start `caribou describe` once per session.\n  caribou-lsp supplies the same declarations in process instead, and keeps\n  Bridge.use() out of the editor's session.\n- **`caribou describe` covers every language but Haxe.** It produces a\n  `ModuleDesc` for Wren (from the parse tree, `#export` and inference), for\n  Zyntax frontends (with nothing compiled), and for plugins (by dlopen). It\n  describes Haxe only from compiled HL bytecode. Nothing describes a Haxe\n  module from its source.\n- **WrenLift's server is a binary.** `wlift-lsp` (crates/wlift_lsp) runs on\n  tower-lsp. Its parser, sema, docs and diagnostics are public in the\n  `wren_lift` library. Its sema binds imported names without checking that\n  the module exists.\n- **Zyntax frontends have no editor support.** The Python and Lua frontends\n  stop at the first error, which carries a byte span. Python's\n  `HostResolver` is where a module of another language is supplied to it.\n- **Structured diagnostics are the adapters' side of the same need**: git-bug\n  f570765965bed74b0f2abdb161479905c4ea3330fd9035bb89257f3547d3262d.\n\n\nThe child issues follow, in order. Each cites this issue.","files":null}]}